        "other_parliamentary_business": [
            {
                "body": "The Government has responded to a public (paper) petition calling for a clear standards framework for infrastructure adoption, ensure housing developers, local authorities and water utilities work together to achieve timely infrastructure adoption, and, where necessary, legislate to secure adoption. The petition was presented to the House of Commons by Andrew Cooper MP on behalf of residents of the constituency of Mid Cheshire.\n\n* Read the petition and the Government's response[1]\n\nThe petition asks the House of Commons to develop a clear standards framework for infrastructure adoption, ensure housing developers, local authorities and water utilities work together to achieve timely infrastructure adoption, and, where necessary, legislate to secure adoption.\n\nIn response to the petition's request, the Government states that it recognises concerns about delays in the adoption of roads, sewers, drainage systems, green spaces and other shared infrastructure on new housing estates, which can leave homeowners responsible for maintenance costs through private estate management arrangements. To address this, it launched a consultation on 18 December 2025 on reducing the use of these arrangements in England, seeking views on increasing adoption by public authorities and utility companies, introducing common standards, considering mandatory adoption of certain infrastructure, removing incentives for non-adoption, and improving transparency. \n\nThe Government is also commissioning research into road adoption processes and considering lessons from Welsh legislation that supports earlier approval and adoption of drainage and sewerage infrastructure. Alongside this, planning policy already requires sustainable drainage systems (SuDS) on new developments, with new National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) requirements introducing national design standards. The Government will consider consultation responses before setting out next steps, with the aim of reducing privately managed estates, improving the quality and consistency of adoptable infrastructure, and ensuring residents are not left facing avoidable costs or uncertain responsibilities.\n\nWhat are public (paper) petitions?\n\nA public (paper) petition is a petition to the House of Commons presented by an MP.\n\n* Find out more about public (paper) petitions[2]\n\nGet involved in the work of the UK Parliament\n\nYou can also sign up[3] to the UK Parliament newsletter for the latest information on how to get involved and make a difference.\n\n[1]: https://hansard.parliament.uk/Commons/2026-10-12/debates/26101231000208/InfrastructureAdoption\n[2]: https://www.parliament.uk/get-involved/sign-a-petition/paper-petitions/\n[3]: https://parliament.us14.list-manage.com/subscribe?u=098af33234df9814f59fb205f&id=6e29cb1262",
                "created_at": "2026-10-30T15:06:32.100Z",
